# CS193V course container.
#
# Build:   ./cs193v --rebuild      (students and staff alike; wraps podman build)
#
# THIS FILE IS THE DISTRIBUTION. There is no registry and no published image: every
# student builds this on their own machine at install time. That makes the pins below
# load-bearing in a way they were not when a CI job baked one artifact for everyone —
# see "Pinned inputs" underneath the ARGs.
#
# LAYER ORDERING IS DELIBERATE, and it now serves a different purpose than it was written
# for. It was written when students PULLED this image: podman cannot resume a partial layer
# download, so putting the most volatile software last meant a dropped connection cost at
# most one layer. Building locally, the same ordering buys BUILD-CACHE reuse — measured,
# bumping CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION costs 89 s and 95 MB against 224 s and 728 MB cold. A killed
# build also resumes at the failed step, which is finer-grained than layer-level pull
# resume ever was.
#
# THAT PAYOFF DEPENDS ENTIRELY ON WHERE THE VERSION ARGs SIT -- see the note above each of
# them. With all four in a block at the top, the identical bump cost 250 s and 726 MB and
# re-ran 18 of 23 steps, because an in-scope ARG is part of every later step's cache key.
# Ordering alone buys nothing. 10-static.sh asserts the placement.
#
# `####>` LINES ARE THE PROGRESS METER'S SIDE TEXT, not ordinary comments. Each one names the
# instruction below it and every instruction after that until the next marker, so a student
# watching `./cs193v --rebuild` reads "Installing Chromium..." beside the bar instead of nothing.
# The launcher parses them out of this file (see CF_PARSE_AWK in ../cs193v) because podman
# does not emit comments and does not re-run a cached RUN, so there is no way to get this text
# out of the build itself.
#
# Three rules, all enforced by 10-static.sh:
#   - a marker sits directly above a top-level instruction, never inside a `\` continuation;
#   - the first one is above FROM, so the label is never blank;
#   - no heredocs and no `# escape=` anywhere in this file -- the parser counts instructions
#     line by line, and either one would shift every label after it.
# Rewording one is free logic-wise, but it changes cs193v.buildhash and so prompts every
# student to rebuild. Batch reword with a change that warrants a rebuild anyway.
#
# Everything below is deliberate; see CONTAINER-DESIGN.md for the reasoning.

# Pinned by MANIFEST-LIST digest, not a per-architecture one, so this single line serves
# both the arm64 leg (every supported Mac, and Windows-on-ARM) and the amd64 leg. podman
# resolves the list and picks the student's own architecture.
#
# The `26.04` TAG MOVES. Ubuntu republishes it whenever the base rolls forward, so on the
# tag alone two students installing a week apart would build on different base images —
# the one drift this file can close in a single line, and the reason it is closed here.
#
# THE TAG IS DECORATION. In `name:tag@digest` the digest wins outright and the tag is not
# checked against it, so editing `26.04` here changes nothing at all — a future release
# bump that touches only the tag would silently keep building 26.04. It is kept because
# `FROM ubuntu@sha256:678c...` alone tells a reader nothing about what this is. Change
# both, or neither.
#
# Getting a new one, both parts:
#     skopeo inspect --raw docker://docker.io/library/ubuntu:<tag> | sha256sum
# Note that `podman manifest inspect` and `skopeo` both REFUSE a `tag@digest` reference
# ("references with both a tag and digest are currently not supported"), which is why
# 00-release-gates.sh strips the tag before it verifies this line.
####> Downloading the base image...
FROM ubuntu:26.04@sha256:678c6550cc43645e08669028bc177f50be4e7c5b8cca677067b1914d4afc7a03

# ─── Pinned inputs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All four are exact, and none of them may be `latest`. These used to be CI's job: the
# published image was built once with explicit versions and every student pulled that one
# artifact, so a floating ARG only meant CI drifted between runs. Now each student runs
# this build themselves, and a floating ARG means TWO STUDENTS IN THE SAME LAB SECTION
# GET DIFFERENT SOFTWARE. `00-release-gates.sh` §3 enforces it.
#
# The rationale for each individual pin sits beside that pin, not here — see below.
#
# EACH VERSION ARG IS DECLARED IMMEDIATELY BEFORE THE LAYER THAT USES IT, not in a block up
# here, and that placement is load-bearing rather than tidiness. MEASURED: with all four
# declared here, bumping CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION re-ran 18 of 23 steps -- 250 s and 726 MB, no
# cheaper than a cold build. buildah puts every in-scope build arg into each step's cache
# key (you can see it in the step headers: `|5 CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION=... DEBIAN_FRONTEND=...`),
# so an ARG declared at the top invalidates the cache for everything after it. The layer
# ordering below cannot help, because the ARG fires before any of those layers do.
#
# Declared at the point of use, a bump invalidates only that layer onward, which is what the
# ordering was designed to give. Moving one of these back up here silently costs a student
# a full rebuild for a one-line version bump, and nothing would fail to warn you.
#
# DEBIAN_FRONTEND is the exception that proves it: it stays global because every apt layer
# needs it and its value never changes, so it never invalidates anything.
####> Installing system packages...
ARG DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 1 — system packages
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RUN apt-get update \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      ca-certificates curl wget \
      git build-essential \
      python3 python3-pip python3-dev python3-venv pipx \
      nano less sudo uidmap \
      procps psmisc file unzip zip xz-utils inotify-tools \
      locales tzdata \
      fonts-noto-core fontconfig \
      openssh-server openssh-client inetutils-telnet \
      iproute2 lsof \
      tmux ncurses-term \
 && locale-gen en_US.UTF-8 \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
# THE PYTHON SPLIT is the whole of what this image decides about Python, and it is three lines:
# the INTERPRETER is apt's and root's, IMPORTABLE LIBRARIES are the student's and pip's, in
# ~/.local/lib/python3.14/site-packages, and PROGRAMS are apt's -- or pipx into /usr/local where
# apt has no package, as tldr is further down. That is the same split layer 2 makes between
# /usr/bin/node and the student's own npm prefix.
#
# SO NO LIBRARY IS PREINSTALLED, and python3-numpy was REMOVED rather than joined by pandas.
# Nothing in this image ever imported it -- rewrite-window-title.py imports sys and nothing else
# -- and one apt library sitting beside a pip-installed set is the state that misleads: `pip
# list` reports a version pip cannot upgrade in place, and `pip install -U numpy` leaves apt's
# copy shadowed but still on disk. Measured, if a set is ever wanted here: pandas, matplotlib,
# requests and openpyxl are 247 MB in the student's user site and 11 s to install on demand.
# Issue #44 weighed those against each other and chose on demand; the staff README records what
# that gives up (a --rebuild wipes them, offline installs fail, versions drift between students)
# and where the layer would go if it comes back.
#
# python3-dev buys the one thing pip cannot download: Python.h. Without it a source build dies
# at `fatal error: Python.h: No such file or directory` -- verified against psutil, which then
# compiles. It is insurance for the tail rather than the common case, because scipy,
# scikit-learn, pillow, lxml, duckdb, polars, seaborn, jupyterlab, notebook, ipython and bs4 all
# ship cp314 wheels for this base image's Python 3.14.
#
# ITS COST IS PAID BY NUMPY LEAVING, measured rather than assumed: python3-dev pulls 39.2 MB
# (libpython3.14-dev 28 MB and the libpython3.14 shared library this image did not have, plus
# zlib1g-dev and libexpat1-dev), and dropping python3-numpy freed 39.2 MB -- 27 MB of numpy and
# 11.7 MB of the libblas3, liblapack3 and libgfortran5 that Debian's numpy links against and that
# nothing else here wanted. Installed size moved by -20 KB; the image came out 14.3 MB SMALLER
# (2,165,775,565 against 2,180,064,389 bytes).
#
# python3-venv is NAMED here for the reason openssh-client is: it has been in every image ever
# built, but only because pipx Depends on it. If pipx ever left, `python3 -m venv` would leave
# with it and the only symptom would be a lab exercise that had quietly stopped working.
#
# python3-setuptools is deliberately ABSENT. pip's default PEP 517 build isolation downloads its
# own backend, so a source build needs no system copy, and Debian de-vendors setuptools into 11
# packages (python3-jaraco.text, python3-inflect, python3-typeguard ...) -- the same divergence
# from what a student reads that keeps Ubuntu's own nodejs out of layer 2. The price is
# exact and small: `pip install --no-build-isolation` fails with `BackendUnavailable: Cannot
# import 'setuptools.build_meta'`, and `import pkg_resources` is a ModuleNotFoundError, as are
# `import setuptools` and `import distutils` -- here and in a fresh venv alike, since Python 3.12
# bundles none of them. `pip3 install setuptools` fixes either one in two seconds with no sudo.
#
# tmux is the student's landing point: `./cs193v` runs cs193v-shell, which puts them inside
# a locked-down tmux rather than a bare bash. See files/tmux/tmux.conf for the whole of that
# configuration and files/cs193v-shell for how a session is chosen. It is in Ubuntu main on
# both 24.04 and 26.04, all architectures, maintained by Ubuntu's security team, which is
# why a multiplexer is an apt line here rather than a vendored binary.
#
# inotify-tools is here for a test before it is here for a student, and that is the honest order.
# CONTAINER-DESIGN.md tells students their dev server's hot reload works for edits made inside
# the container, and 60-container.sh's files:inotify-fires-for-container-side-edits is the only
# check of that claim anywhere -- but the check guarded itself on `command -v inotifywait`, the
# package was not in this image, and so it had never once run (#39). A property the course
# documents and nothing measures is the part worth paying for; the package is 34 KB plus its
# library. `inotifywait` is a reasonable thing to hand a student who asks why their watcher is not
# firing, which is the second reason and not the deciding one.
#
# ncurses-term is listed EXPLICITLY, for the same reason fontconfig is a few lines up: it is
# only a Recommends, this apt line uses --no-install-recommends, and nothing hard-depends on
# it. It carries the `tmux-256color` terminfo entry -- ncurses-base ships only a handful of
# common entries and that is not among them. Without it tmux refuses to start at all:
# `default-terminal "tmux-256color"` becomes "missing or unsuitable terminal", which with
# tmux as the landing point means no student can open a shell. The build-time check further
# down would catch it, which is the point of having one.
# openssh-server provides the sshd that serves the loopback tunnel. It is a SERVER package
# for a server that never listens: the launcher starts it per session as
# `podman exec -i ... sshd -i`, so its connection is a pipe, not a socket. See
# files/sshd_config, and the Networking section of .config/container.args for why the tunnel
# exists at all.
#
# openssh-client and inetutils-telnet are here FOR THE STUDENT, and they overturn what this
# comment used to say. It claimed the client lived on the host only, that nothing in the
# container ever initiates an ssh connection, and that the asymmetry was the security
# argument. Two things were wrong with that.
#
# It was not TRUE. openssh-server Depends on openssh-client, so `ssh`, `scp` and `sftp` have
# been in every image ever built — by accident rather than by decision, and documented as
# absent while they were present.
#
# And it is not what the course wants. Logging into a remote machine, copying a file across
# with scp, and typing an HTTP request at a web server by hand are things a systems course
# teaches, not things it withholds. Issue #2.
#
# The security argument does not survive contact with the rest of the image either: there is
# no egress filtering (deliberately — see the staff README), and curl, wget and git are all
# here already. A student who can `curl` anything on the internet is not newly dangerous for
# also being able to `ssh` to it.
#
# They are named EXPLICITLY rather than left to the dependency. A transitive dependency is
# not a promise: if the tunnel ever stopped needing sshd, `ssh` and `scp` would vanish from
# the image and the only sign would be a lab exercise that had quietly stopped working.
#
# inetutils-telnet, not `telnet`: on Ubuntu 26.04 `telnet` is a transitional dummy package
# whose entire content is a dependency on this one, and transitional packages get removed.
#
# iproute2 and lsof answer the question this course asks more than any other: "my browser
# cannot reach my server -- what is it actually listening on?" The base image ships neither,
# and without them the only way to see a listening socket is to read /proc/net/tcp and
# /proc/net/tcp6 and decode the addresses by hand: little-endian hex, and four byte-swapped
# 32-bit words for IPv6. That is not something to ask a student to do, and it is not
# something to ask an agent to reinvent per session either -- `ss -ltn` is two packages and
# the answer everything on the internet will tell them to type.
#
# lsof as well as ss because they answer different halves. `ss -ltnp` names the bind address,
# which is the half that decides whether the tunnel can reach it; `lsof -i :3000` names the
# process, which is the half that says what to stop. Neither is inferable from the other.
#
# fonts-noto-core, not fonts-dejavu-core: the base image ships no fonts at all, and
# anything that rasterizes text (Pillow, matplotlib, librsvg) needs one. Noto because a
# large share of the web uses it, so rendered output looks unremarkable rather than
# unmistakably-Linux. Terminal emoji and CJK come from the HOST terminal's fonts.
#
# fontconfig is listed EXPLICITLY because fonts-noto-core only Recommends it, and this
# apt line uses --no-install-recommends. Without it there is no fc-list or fc-match, so
# nothing that resolves fonts through fontconfig — librsvg, Pango, GD, ImageMagick — can
# find the 271 Noto files, and the image pays 42 MB for fonts nothing can enumerate.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The student account. uid/gid 1000 is the Debian/Ubuntu convention for the first
# human user, which makes --userns=keep-id line up on Ubuntu and WSL without
# translation. Passwordless sudo is a deliberate course decision: CS193V trains
# students not to run commands on their host, so system changes must be possible
# from inside. See CONTAINER-DESIGN.md § "What sudo costs you".
#
# The pre-existing occupant of 1000 has to go first. Since 23.04 the Ubuntu base image
# ships its own `ubuntu` user at uid AND gid 1000, so a bare `groupadd -g 1000` fails
# with "GID '1000' already exists" and exits 4, which aborts the build. Deleting it is
# right rather than reusing it: the account is named in every prompt, path and error a
# student reads, and `--userns=keep-id:uid=1000,gid=1000` pins the number, so the name
# has to be ours.
#
# Guarded with getent rather than assumed, so this keeps working on a future base image
# that drops the default user — and userdel removes the primary group with the user when
# nothing else uses it, which is why the group check comes after and is separate.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
####> Creating the student account...
RUN set -eux; \
    if getent passwd 1000 >/dev/null; then \
      existing_user="$(getent passwd 1000 | cut -d: -f1)"; \
      userdel -r "$existing_user" 2>/dev/null || userdel "$existing_user"; \
    fi; \
    if getent group 1000 >/dev/null; then \
      groupdel "$(getent group 1000 | cut -d: -f1)"; \
    fi; \
    groupadd -g 1000 student; \
    useradd -u 1000 -g 1000 -m -s /bin/bash student; \
    printf 'student ALL=(root) NOPASSWD:ALL\n' > /etc/sudoers.d/90-student; \
    chmod 0440 /etc/sudoers.d/90-student; \
    test "$(id -u student)" = 1000; \
    test "$(id -g student)" = 1000

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 2 — Node, from NodeSource's apt repository.
#
# apt-managed on purpose, so `apt upgrade` inside the container picks up Node security
# fixes. A tarball unpacked into /usr/local cannot be updated by anything a student runs:
# every CVE would need an image rebuild and a NODE_VERSION bump, and until that shipped
# there would be no way to patch it from inside.
#
# NodeSource rather than Ubuntu's own nodejs, because Ubuntu 26.04 carries node 22.22.1
# with npm 9.2.0 — two majors behind on both — and de-bundles npm's vendored dependencies
# into ~70 separate node-* packages, which diverges from what every tutorial a student
# reads will do. NodeSource's nodistro suite carries amd64 and arm64, so one repo line
# serves both legs of the manifest.
#
# NOT nvm. The devcontainer's nvm tree is drwxrwsr-x vscode:nvm — group-writable AND
# setgid — so an agent can trojan the shared node/npm install with NO sudo at all, which
# makes it the quietest tampering path available. apt installs root-owned into /usr/bin and
# /usr/lib/node_modules, which removes that by construction.
#
# The exact patch version is pinned for a reproducible build, but the package is
# deliberately NOT apt-mark hold'd — holding it would re-create the problem the switch away
# from the tarball was meant to solve.
#
# The key is stored armored as .asc, which apt reads directly, so this needs no gnupg.
#
# THE STUDENT'S NPM PREFIX IS SET HERE, in the same layer, because every `npm install -g`
# below depends on it and npm reads exactly ONE global prefix. Root's is nodesource's /usr;
# the student's is ~/.local. Setting it after those installs -- which is where it used to
# live, down in layer 8 -- meant the tools went to /usr as root while the student read
# ~/.local, so `npm ls -g` never saw them and Claude Code's updater wrote a second copy into
# ~/.local that shadowed the first on PATH. See issue #13.
#
# ~/.local/lib/node_modules is pre-created rather than left to the first install. An npm
# prefix whose lib/ does not exist makes `npm ls -g` EXIT 254 with an ENOENT stack and a
# debug-log path instead of printing an empty tree -- which is what a student got before the
# installs moved here, and would get again on any prefix nothing had written to yet.
#
# THE PIN ITSELF is verified rather than assumed: NodeSource's nodistro suite retains the
# whole 24.x patch history (24.0.0 onward as of this pin), so unlike a distro archive that
# drops superseded packages, this exact-version pin does not expire out from under a student
# who installs in week 6 -- which matters now that students build rather than pull. Re-check
# with `apt-cache madison nodejs` before assuming it of a future major.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
####> Installing Node...
ARG NODE_VERSION=24.18.1
RUN set -eux; \
    install -d -m 0755 /etc/apt/keyrings; \
    curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/gpgkey/nodesource-repo.gpg.key \
      -o /etc/apt/keyrings/nodesource.asc; \
    chmod go+r /etc/apt/keyrings/nodesource.asc; \
    printf 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/nodesource.asc] https://deb.nodesource.com/node_%s.x nodistro main\n' \
      "${NODE_VERSION%%.*}" > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/nodesource.list; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends "nodejs=${NODE_VERSION}-1nodesource1"; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    test "$(node --version)" = "v${NODE_VERSION}"; \
    node --version; npm --version; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.local; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.local/bin; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.local/lib; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.local/lib/node_modules; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'npm config set prefix /home/student/.local'; \
    test "$(su student -s /bin/sh -c 'npm config get prefix')" = /home/student/.local; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'npm ls -g --depth=0' > /dev/null

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 3 — GitHub CLI
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
####> Installing the GitHub CLI...
RUN set -eux; \
    mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings; \
    curl -fsSL https://cli.github.com/packages/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
      -o /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg; \
    chmod go+r /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg; \
    printf 'deb [arch=%s signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg] https://cli.github.com/packages stable main\n' \
      "$(dpkg --print-architecture)" > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/github-cli.list; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends gh; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    gh --version

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 4 — Playwright's CLI, and Chromium's system libraries
#
# The image ships a browser because the course's test harnesses are browser tests: Claude
# Code writes them on the student's behalf and `npm test` runs them. Nothing else here
# needed one, which is why `puppeteer and Chrome` were on the rejected list until now.
#
# THE LIBRARIES ARE THE HALF THAT HAS TO BE BAKED. Not one of the 21 packages Chromium
# needs is in this base image — measured with dpkg, not assumed — so leaving them out would
# put a sudo-and-apt detour in the middle of `npm test`: it needs root, it needs the Ubuntu
# archives, and /usr/lib is in no volume and no bind mount, so every --rebuild would destroy
# it again. The browser itself is the cheap half; it lands in a cache that IS a volume.
#
# `install-deps` rather than a hand-copied apt list: the list belongs to the pinned version,
# so it cannot drift from the browser it serves, and playwright runs exactly the
# `apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends` this file would have written by hand.
#
# PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD is deliberately NOT set. The feature this replaces needed
# PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD because puppeteer fetches a browser from its install hook;
# playwright@1.62.1 declares no `scripts` at all, so `npm install -g` downloads nothing and
# the guard would be cargo cult.
#
# `npm cache clean --force` in the same layer, for the reason spelled out on the Vercel
# layer below -- as the STUDENT, since that is now whose cache gets written.
#
# THE TWO HALVES RUN AS DIFFERENT USERS, deliberately. The npm install goes to the student's
# prefix (issue #13, see layer 2), but `install-deps` is an apt install and needs root, which
# is the layer's own user. Splitting them across two layers would be the obvious alternative
# and is worse: the library set belongs to the pinned playwright version, so they must
# invalidate together.
#
# The binary is named by absolute path, not found on PATH: the ENV that puts
# ~/.local/bin first is in layer 8, several layers below this, and `su` resets PATH anyway.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pinned to an exact patch rather than the `1.62` range the devcontainer feature used, and
# never `latest`: this version chooses the Chromium build, so a floating pin would quietly
# change which browser every student gets.
#
# 1.61.0 is a FLOOR, not a preference. Playwright's platform table gained `ubuntu26.04` in
# 1.61.0; 1.60 and earlier resolve this base image to ubuntu24.04 and are unsupported on it.
# Verified by grepping playwright-core's bundle: 0 hits in 1.58 and 1.60, 28 in 1.61 and
# 1.62.1. The devcontainer this came from is on Ubuntu 24.04 and so never met this limit.
####> Installing Playwright...
ARG PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION=1.62.1
RUN set -eux; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c "npm install -g 'playwright@${PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION}'"; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'npm cache clean --force'; \
    /home/student/.local/bin/playwright --version; \
    /home/student/.local/bin/playwright install-deps chromium; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 5 — the Chromium headless shell, and the proof that it works
#
# --only-shell: the headless shell is what playwright drives for a headless run anyway.
# Measured at this revision, the shell is a 114 MB download and the full Chrome for Testing
# build is another 184 MB on top of it — `playwright install chromium` fetches both. There is
# no display in this container, so the headed browser those 184 MB buy could never be watched.
#
# Installed AS THE STUDENT, which is load-bearing twice: the runtime path is the student's
# own cache, and podman's first-mount copy of this directory into the cs193v-playwright
# volume inherits whatever ownership it finds here.
#
# PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH is set NOWHERE — not as ENV, not inline. Playwright's default is
# ~/.cache/ms-playwright, which is both the path the volume covers and the path a
# project-local @playwright/test searches with no configuration. Setting it would break the
# one workflow this exists for.
#
# The three checks come from the feature this replaces, where they were written after a
# wrong-architecture browser shipped (puppeteer#7740): the binary is for THIS cpu, it
# executes, and playwright can actually drive it. A missing library shows up only in the
# last one — the first two pass happily on an image whose Chromium cannot start.
#
# The `find` covers BOTH layouts deliberately. The feature globbed
# chrome-headless-shell-linux*/chrome-headless-shell, which exists on x86-64 only: Chrome for
# Testing has no linux-arm64 leg at all, so on arm64 playwright ships its own build and
# unpacks it to chrome-linux/headless_shell. The glob finds nothing there, so it would have
# failed the arm64 half of a multi-arch build with a message blaming provisioning.
#
# The probe is written and deleted in THIS layer. Deleted in a later one, it would still ship.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
####> Installing Chromium...
RUN set -eux; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c '/home/student/.local/bin/playwright install --only-shell chromium'; \
    CHROME_BIN="$(find /home/student/.cache/ms-playwright -type f \
        \( -name chrome-headless-shell -o -name headless_shell \) -perm -u+x | head -1)"; \
    test -n "$CHROME_BIN"; \
    case "$(uname -m)" in \
      x86_64)  WANT_ELF=3e00 ;; \
      aarch64) WANT_ELF=b700 ;; \
      *) echo "FATAL: no Chromium is provisioned for $(uname -m)" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    test "$(od -An -t x1 -j 18 -N 2 -- "$CHROME_BIN" | tr -d ' \n')" = "$WANT_ELF"; \
    timeout 60 "$CHROME_BIN" --version; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'timeout 120 /home/student/.local/bin/playwright screenshot \
        -b chromium about:blank /home/student/.cache/chromium-probe.png'; \
    test -s /home/student/.cache/chromium-probe.png; \
    rm -f /home/student/.cache/chromium-probe.png

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 6 — Vercel CLI
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `npm cache clean --force` in the same layer, not a later one: a file deleted in a later
# layer still ships in the earlier one. The npm cache is dead weight in something students
# download over dorm wifi, and it was inflating this layer and the next by roughly 150 MB
# each. It is the STUDENT's cache that has to be cleaned now -- /home/student/.npm, not
# /root/.npm -- because the install runs as the student. 50-image.sh measures both.
####> Installing the Vercel CLI...
ARG VERCEL_VERSION=58.8.0
RUN set -eux; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c "npm install -g 'vercel@${VERCEL_VERSION}'"; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'npm cache clean --force'; \
    /home/student/.local/bin/vercel --version

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 7 — OpenAI Codex
#
# BEFORE Claude Code, not after, and that ordering is a cache-cost decision rather than a
# preference. A bump re-runs its own layer plus every layer below it, and these are the two
# biggest layers in the file -- MEASURED with `podman history`, not estimated: codex 312 MB,
# Claude Code 298 MB, and ~25 MB of config and tldr-cache layers after them.
#
# So the question is only which tool's bump is the cheap one. Claude Code's pin is the one most
# often moved, and with codex ABOVE it that bump re-runs ~323 MB; with codex below, the same bump
# would drag codex's 312 MB along for ~635 MB. The codex bump pays that price instead, which is
# the right way round while Claude Code is the pin that moves. 10-static.sh asserts the order.
#
# (Do not compare those layer sizes against the "89 s and 95 MB" for a CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION bump
# at the top of this file: that figure is the DELTA podman reports for the bump, not the size of
# the layer it rebuilds. Cold build of this recipe, measured on the development machine: 242 s.)
#
# THE npm PACKAGE IS A WRAPPER. `@openai/codex` is three files and 11 KB: a node script that
# spawns a native binary out of a platform optionalDependency (`@openai/codex-linux-x64` or
# `-linux-arm64`, aliased to this exact version, so pinning the wrapper pins the binary too).
# Two consequences elsewhere in this repo: /proc reports the INTERPRETER for a `#!/usr/bin/env
# node` script, which is why files/tmux/tabname.bash lists `codex` for the same reason it lists
# `claude`; and the vendored tree carries its own `rg` and `zsh` off the student's PATH, so the
# "ripgrep is deliberately absent" decision in the staff README is untouched.
#
# INSTALLED AS THE STUDENT for the reason in layer 2, and codex makes the same demand Claude
# Code does: the wrapper detects npm (it sets CODEX_MANAGED_BY_NPM=1) and its update advice is
# `npm install -g @openai/codex@latest`, which writes whatever the STUDENT's prefix points at.
# Installed as root it would land in nodesource's /usr prefix, invisible to `npm ls -g`, and an
# update would put a SECOND 309 MB copy in ~/.local that shadows this one on PATH. Issue #13.
#
# THE VERSION CHECK RUNS AS THE STUDENT, unlike the vercel and claude checks just below and
# above, which run as root by absolute path. Two reasons: it is what proves the binary is
# reachable and executable from the account that will actually run it (issue #71 asks for
# exactly that), and running it as root would leave a stray /root/.codex in the image.
#
# THE ELF CHECK is layer 5's, for layer 5's reason. npm picks the platform package from `os`
# and `cpu` fields, and if that selection is ever wrong the symptom is a wrong-architecture
# binary that installs perfectly happily -- which is what the Chromium check was written for
# after puppeteer#7740.
#
# BUBBLEWRAP IS HERE AND NOT ON LAYER 1'S apt LINE, which will look like untidiness to whoever
# reads this next. It is a build-cost decision: layer 1 sits above everything, so adding a
# package there turns any future change to this file into a full ~2.5 GB rebuild for every
# student instead of re-running the layers from here down. Layer 4 already establishes the shape
# -- an apt install as root in a later layer, beside an npm install as the student.
#
# What it buys is exact, and it is not a sandbox. codex ships its own vendored bwrap but prefers
# a system one, warning at every start when there is none: "Codex could not find system
# bubblewrap at /usr/bin/bwrap. Please install bubblewrap with your package manager." In THIS
# container that warning is worse than noise, because a student can run the `sudo apt install`
# it suggests and the next --rebuild silently undoes it. The package also takes codex's
# documented "improved sandbox startup on Ubuntu/AppArmor hosts" path, which matters because
# no sandbox policy is shipped (see below) and codex therefore runs on its `workspace-write`
# default and really does invoke bwrap.
#
# THE SANDBOX POLICY IS SHIPPED, in /etc/codex/managed_config.toml, installed by layer 9 -- the
# analogue of /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json, and in /etc for the same two reasons:
# ~/.codex is a named volume, so a file placed there is seeded once and never refreshed, and the
# student can edit it. See files/codex/managed_config.toml for what each key is for; the short
# version is `workspace-write` + `on-request` (codex's own defaults, written down so they cannot
# drift under us) and `approvals_reviewer = "auto_review"`, which is a deliberate divergence from
# the Claude Code policy beside it and is recorded as one.
#
# TWO THINGS THAT BITE, both verified against the real binary rather than reasoned about:
#
#   * codex IGNORES A KEY IT DOES NOT RECOGNISE, in silence. A bogus key leaves `codex doctor`
#     reporting "config loaded" with no warning, so a typo or an upstream rename is a policy that
#     does nothing and says nothing. Layer 9 parses the TOML, 10-static.sh pins the exact key set
#     and values, and 50-image.sh proves codex really reads /etc/codex by overriding it with a
#     DIFFERENT value and watching doctor's output change. That last one is the only assertion of
#     the three that could not pass against an image carrying no policy at all.
#   * NOTHING CAN ASSERT approvals_reviewer. `codex doctor`, even with --all, never names the
#     reviewer, so no test here can tell `user` from `auto_review`; only a live escalation shows
#     who answers. MANUAL.md carries that check.
#
# These are managed DEFAULTS, not hard constraints: no requirements.toml is shipped, and a
# student's own ~/.codex/config.toml lives in a volume that --rebuild does NOT clear (only
# --rebuild --logout does), so anything they set there survives a rebuild.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
####> Installing Codex...
ARG CODEX_VERSION=0.148.0
RUN set -eux; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends bubblewrap; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    bwrap --version; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c "npm install -g '@openai/codex@${CODEX_VERSION}'"; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'npm cache clean --force'; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c '/home/student/.local/bin/codex --version' | grep -qF "${CODEX_VERSION}"; \
    CODEX_BIN="$(find /home/student/.local/lib/node_modules/@openai -type f -name codex -perm -u+x | head -1)"; \
    test -n "$CODEX_BIN"; \
    case "$(uname -m)" in \
      x86_64)  WANT_ELF=3e00 ;; \
      aarch64) WANT_ELF=b700 ;; \
      *) echo "FATAL: no Codex is provisioned for $(uname -m)" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    test "$(od -An -t x1 -j 18 -N 2 -- "$CODEX_BIN" | tr -d ' \n')" = "$WANT_ELF"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 8 — Claude Code.  MOST VOLATILE: keep it last so a version bump
# invalidates only this layer and the small config layer after it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Installed as the student for the reason in layer 2, and this is the layer where it matters
# most: Claude Code's auto-updater is left enabled on purpose, it detects this as an
# `npm-global` install, and its update path runs `npm install -g` with no `--prefix`. So it
# writes wherever the STUDENT's prefix points. Installed here, that is the same directory the
# image put it in and the update is genuinely in place; installed as root it was a different
# directory, and the update quietly shadowed the image's copy via PATH. Issue #13.
#
# THE PIN is the one most often bumped, and the only one whose partner is checked at build
# time but never verified: /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json is parsed for valid JSON
# further down, which does NOT prove the installed Claude Code accepts its schema. Bumping
# this means re-testing that pair. Declared here rather than at the top of the file so the
# bump does not invalidate the whole build -- see the note beside DEBIAN_FRONTEND.
####> Installing Claude Code...
ARG CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION=2.1.225
RUN set -eux; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c "npm install -g '@anthropic-ai/claude-code@${CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION}'"; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'npm cache clean --force'; \
    /home/student/.local/bin/claude --version

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layer 9 — course configuration
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# tldr, installed system-wide via pipx. Stands in for man pages, which the Ubuntu base
# image strips and which we deliberately do not restore.
#
# PIPX_HOME/PIPX_BIN_DIR are set INLINE, not as ENV: as ENV they would persist into the
# runtime and point a student's own `pipx install` at root-owned /usr/local, where it
# would fail.
####> Installing tldr...
RUN PIPX_HOME=/usr/local/pipx PIPX_BIN_DIR=/usr/local/bin \
    pipx install tldr \
 && tldr --version

####> Adding the course files...
COPY files/ /tmp/cs193v-files/

# NOTE ON STYLE: no `#` comments inside the RUN below. The Dockerfile parser does strip
# comment lines inside a line continuation, but relying on that is fragile — if it ever
# did not apply, a comment line would swallow the command after it and silently produce a
# broken image. So the explanation lives here and the RUN body stays comment-free.
#
# What it does, in order:
#
#  1. Installs the helper commands: the $BROWSER stub, the entrypoint, and the three that
#     make up the landing point --
#     cs193v-shell (what `./cs193v` runs), plus cs193v-welcome and cs193v-goodbye. Those
#     last two are commands rather than inline shell because they now have two callers
#     each: the tmux path and the plain `bash -l` path. See item 9.
#
#     `man` is the odd one out and goes to /usr/bin, not /usr/local/bin, because it
#     REPLACES something rather than adding it. Ubuntu's minimized base leaves a
#     /usr/bin/man of its own that exits 0 and tells the reader to run `unminimize` — an
#     instruction that downloads hundreds of megabytes into a container the next
#     `cs193v --rebuild` discards. Shadowing it from /usr/local/bin would leave that stub
#     reachable as `/usr/bin/man` and to anything with a fixed PATH, so it is overwritten.
#     Manual pages stay deliberately absent and tldr stands in; files/man says exactly that
#     and names the tldr page to try. Issue #8.
#
#  2. Installs the Claude Code course policy into /etc/claude-code — deliberately NOT
#     under ~/.claude, which is a named volume: an image-provided file there is seeded
#     once on first mount and then never refreshed by a later image. /etc is in the image
#     layer, so --rebuild and every image update restore it. The JSON is parsed at build
#     time so a typo fails the build instead of a student's session.
#
#     THE NOTES THEMSELVES ARE ONE FILE FOR TWO AGENTS, and they are not here: they live at
#     /etc/cs193v/agent-notes.md with item 10's other shared files, and /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md
#     is a SYMLINK to them. Codex reads global instructions only from $CODEX_HOME, which is a
#     volume, so files/entrypoint.sh links ~/.codex/AGENTS.md at the same file on every start —
#     the "seeded once, never refreshed" trap this item exists to avoid, met a second time.
#
#     A symlink rather than Claude Code's `@path` import, which is what its docs show first and
#     therefore what the next reader will propose. The import would allow Claude-only text below
#     it, and costs an external-import approval dialog whose behaviour in the MANAGED slot is
#     undocumented — and where a decline is silent, permanent, and takes the notes with it. The
#     docs bless a symlink for exactly this case. Switching later is one line.
#
#     The build-time grep is for an `@word` outside backticks. Claude Code would read one as an
#     import and expand a file into the student's context; codex reads it as prose. It is the one
#     thing in a shared file that breaks differently for each reader.
#
#  3. Turns off XON/XOFF flow control for interactive shells, via both profile.d (login
#     shells) and bash.bashrc (interactive non-login). Ctrl-S is "save" in every GUI
#     editor; on a terminal it freezes all output with no echo, which looks exactly like
#     a crash, and the recovery (Ctrl-Q) is unknown to novices.
#
#  4. Installs the nanorc.
#
#  5. Pre-creates all SEVEN volume mount points STUDENT-OWNED. This is the load-bearing part.
#     ~/.cache/ms-playwright already exists by now — Layer 5 put the browser there — but it
#     is listed here anyway so that one block names every mount point the volume set covers,
#     rather than five of them plus one created as a side effect three layers earlier.
#     podman auto-chowns an EMPTY named volume to the container user on first mount, then
#     re-chowns it to match the image's directory at the mount target if that directory
#     exists — and a root-owned target both yields a root-owned volume AND permanently
#     disables further auto-chown. Getting this right here is what lets the entrypoint
#     run with no root phase and no sudo, which is what the devcontainer's five
#     `sudo chown -R` calls were working around.
#
#  6. (Moved to layer 2.) npm's global prefix, and the ~/.local directories it needs, are
#     set up alongside Node now — every `npm install -g` in this file depends on them, so
#     configuring the prefix down here meant the build-time globals went to root's prefix
#     instead of the student's. See issue #13 and the comment on layer 2.
#
#  7. Removes /etc/gitconfig if the base image has one. git stays completely stock so
#     students meet its real hints and errors. The `git commit` editor trap is closed by
#     EDITOR/VISUAL below, NOT by core.editor: git resolves
#     GIT_EDITOR -> core.editor -> VISUAL -> EDITOR -> vi.
#
#     AND CREATES AN EMPTY ~/.config/git/config, which is the one file here whose EXISTENCE is
#     the whole point rather than its contents. `git config --global` writes
#     $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/config if that file exists and ~/.gitconfig does not, and
#     ~/.gitconfig otherwise — git's own documented rule. So creating it empty moves every
#     --global write, setup-git's and `gh auth setup-git`'s credential helper alike, into the
#     directory the cs193v-git volume mounts. Without it they land in ~/.gitconfig, which is
#     the container's writable layer, which --rebuild destroys: a mid-quarter fix would wipe
#     every student's git identity and their credential helper while their token survived in
#     the gh volume, and the next `git commit` would ask them who they are. Nothing is
#     configured by this file being here; it stays stock and empty until setup-git runs.
#     50-image.sh asserts a --global write really lands in it.
#
#  8. Installs the tunnel's sshd config, and pre-creates ~/.ssh STUDENT-OWNED at 0700.
#     That directory is load-bearing for the same class of reason as item 5, and it was
#     measured rather than assumed: the launcher bind-mounts authorized_keys INTO ~/.ssh, and
#     if the directory does not already exist podman creates the mount's parent itself as
#     ROOT (verified: root:root 0755). A root-owned ~/.ssh would then stop a student from
#     ever writing their own id_ed25519 or known_hosts there. 0700 also keeps sshd's strict
#     permission check happy without relying on the mount's own mode.
#
#     The config is then VALIDATED at build time with `sshd -t`, so a typo fails the build
#     rather than a student's tunnel — the same principle as parsing managed-settings.json
#     above. `sshd -t` also sanity-checks host keys, and the real one is per-machine and
#     mounted at runtime, so a throwaway is generated purely to satisfy that check and
#     deleted in the same layer. It must not survive: a host key baked into the image would
#     be one private key shipped to every student.
#
#  9. Installs the tmux configuration into /etc/cs193v/ and the tab-label hook's source
#     line into /etc/bash.bashrc.
#
#     NOT /etc/tmux.conf. cs193v-shell names the file with an explicit `-f`, which
#     suppresses BOTH of tmux's default paths — /etc/tmux.conf and ~/.tmux.conf. The
#     student-writable one is the reason: tmux lets ~/.tmux.conf win, so a student who
#     created it could re-arm a prefix key and put back the 70 bindings the configuration
#     exists to remove, then ask staff about a terminal nobody can reproduce.
#
#     The hook goes in /etc/bash.bashrc rather than being handed to tmux as a
#     `default-command`, because bash.bashrc reaches every interactive shell and therefore
#     every tab with no tmux involvement. Handing a hooked shell to `new-session` instead
#     is a trap that was measured during prototyping: it reaches the FIRST tab only, so
#     wrapper labels work in tab one and silently nowhere else. It is appended LAST, after
#     10-cs193v-shell.sh, because the hook composes PROMPT_COMMAND and anything assigning
#     that wholesale afterwards would silently drop it.
#
#     VALIDATED at build time, on the same principle as `sshd -t` and the JSON parse above,
#     and with more reason than either: tmux is now the only way in, so a configuration
#     error is not a degraded session, it is no session at all. A server is started on a
#     throwaway socket with the real config and then interrogated — no errors reported, the
#     prefix table empty, all six tab bindings present — and killed in the same layer. It
#     runs as `student` because that is who will run it, and it doubles as proof that the
#     tmux-256color terminfo entry from ncurses-term is actually present, since tmux will
#     not start without it.
#
# 10. Installs setup-git, its message catalogue, and /etc/cs193v/ui.sh — the shared
#     presentation layer the LAUNCHER also sources, out of the checkout. That is why it lives
#     under files/ rather than being inlined anywhere: one definition of box(), the arrow-key
#     menu and msg() serves the launcher and everything in here that draws.
#
#     `bash -n` on both, on the same principle as `sshd -t` and the tmux interrogation above:
#     a syntax error in setup-git is a student who cannot set up GitHub at all, and it costs
#     nothing to find at build time. The catalogue gets a grep for one known key instead,
#     because it has no syntax to check and an empty or truncated copy would otherwise show up
#     as "(missing message: intro)" on the student's first screen.
RUN set -eux; \
    install -m 0755 /tmp/cs193v-files/open-url        /usr/local/bin/open-url; \
    install -m 0755 /tmp/cs193v-files/entrypoint.sh   /usr/local/bin/cs193v-entrypoint; \
    install -m 0755 /tmp/cs193v-files/cs193v-shell    /usr/local/bin/cs193v-shell; \
    install -m 0755 /tmp/cs193v-files/cs193v-welcome  /usr/local/bin/cs193v-welcome; \
    install -m 0755 /tmp/cs193v-files/cs193v-goodbye  /usr/local/bin/cs193v-goodbye; \
    install -m 0755 /tmp/cs193v-files/man             /usr/bin/man; \
    sh -n /usr/bin/man; \
    test -z "$(man git 2>&1 | grep -F unminimize || true)"; \
    install -d -m 0755 /etc/claude-code; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/claude-code/managed-settings.json /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json; \
    python3 -c "import json;json.load(open('/etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json'))"; \
    install -d -m 0755 /etc/codex; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/codex/managed_config.toml /etc/codex/managed_config.toml; \
    python3 -c "import tomllib;tomllib.load(open('/etc/codex/managed_config.toml','rb'))"; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/profile.d/10-cs193v-shell.sh /etc/profile.d/10-cs193v-shell.sh; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/profile.d/20-cs193v-welcome.sh /etc/profile.d/20-cs193v-welcome.sh; \
    install -o student -g student -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/bash_logout /home/student/.bash_logout; \
    python3 /tmp/cs193v-files/rewrite-window-title.py; \
    grep -q 'CS193V Development Environment' /home/student/.bashrc; \
    cat /tmp/cs193v-files/profile.d/10-cs193v-shell.sh >> /etc/bash.bashrc; \
    install -d -m 0755 /etc/cs193v; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/agent-notes.md      /etc/cs193v/agent-notes.md; \
    ln -sfn /etc/cs193v/agent-notes.md /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md; \
    test -r /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md; \
    test -z "$(grep -oE '(^|[[:space:]])@[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+' /etc/cs193v/agent-notes.md || true)"; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/cs193v-strings.sh  /etc/cs193v/strings.sh; \
    sh -n /etc/cs193v/strings.sh; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/cs193v-ui.sh          /etc/cs193v/ui.sh; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/setup-git-messages.txt /etc/cs193v/setup-git-messages.txt; \
    install -m 0755 /tmp/cs193v-files/setup-git             /usr/local/bin/setup-git; \
    bash -n /etc/cs193v/ui.sh; \
    bash -n /usr/local/bin/setup-git; \
    test "$(grep -c '^\[\[intro\]\]$' /etc/cs193v/setup-git-messages.txt)" = 1; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/tmux/tmux.conf     /etc/cs193v/tmux.conf; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/tmux/tabname.bash  /etc/cs193v/tabname.bash; \
    printf '\n# CS193V tab labels inside tmux. Must stay last: it composes PROMPT_COMMAND.\nif [ -n "${TMUX:-}" ]; then . /etc/cs193v/tabname.bash; fi\n' >> /etc/bash.bashrc; \
    bash -n /etc/cs193v/tabname.bash; \
    infocmp tmux-256color > /dev/null; \
    su student -s /bin/bash -c 'tmux -L cs193vbuild -f /etc/cs193v/tmux.conf new-session -d -x 80 -y 24 "sleep 60"'; \
    test -z "$(su student -s /bin/bash -c 'tmux -L cs193vbuild show-messages' | grep -F 'tmux.conf' || true)"; \
    test -z "$(su student -s /bin/bash -c 'tmux -L cs193vbuild list-keys -T prefix' 2>/dev/null || true)"; \
    test -z "$(su student -s /bin/bash -c 'tmux -L cs193vbuild list-keys -T copy-mode-vi' 2>/dev/null || true)"; \
    test "$(su student -s /bin/bash -c 'tmux -L cs193vbuild list-keys -T root' | grep -cE ' (M-t|C-t|M-Left|S-Left|M-Right|S-Right) ')" = 6; \
    test "$(su student -s /bin/bash -c 'tmux -L cs193vbuild show -gv destroy-unattached')" = off; \
    su student -s /bin/bash -c 'tmux -L cs193vbuild kill-server'; \
    install -o student -g student -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/nanorc /home/student/.nanorc; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0700 /home/student/.claude; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0700 /home/student/.codex; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0700 /home/student/.claude-json; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.config; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0700 /home/student/.config/gh; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.config/git; \
    install -o student -g student -m 0644 /dev/null /home/student/.config/git/config; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.local/share; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0700 /home/student/.local/share/com.vercel.cli; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.cache; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/.cache/ms-playwright; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0755 /home/student/projects; \
    install -d -o student -g student -m 0700 /home/student/.ssh; \
    install -m 0644 /tmp/cs193v-files/sshd_config /etc/ssh/cs193v_sshd_config; \
    ssh-keygen -q -t ed25519 -N '' -f /etc/ssh/cs193v_host_ed25519_key; \
    /usr/sbin/sshd -t -f /etc/ssh/cs193v_sshd_config; \
    rm -f /etc/ssh/cs193v_host_ed25519_key /etc/ssh/cs193v_host_ed25519_key.pub; \
    rm -f /etc/gitconfig; \
    rm -rf /tmp/cs193v-files

# Populate tldr's cache as the student so it works offline on first use.
#
# NOT `|| true`. `man` is deliberately absent from this image, which makes tldr the only
# command-line help a student has — so an empty cache means no help at all, and a build that
# tolerated a failed fetch would ship exactly that without saying a word. A network hiccup in
# CI is precisely the case this has to catch.
#
# The page count is asserted in the SAME layer as the fetch, so the build cannot succeed with
# an empty or half-written cache. The real cache holds ~7400 pages; 1000 is a floor low
# enough not to be brittle if upstream reorganises, high enough that a failed or truncated
# fetch cannot pass.
####> Caching the tldr pages...
RUN set -eux; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'tldr --update'; \
    test "$(find /home/student/.cache/tldr -type f | wc -l)" -gt 1000; \
    su student -s /bin/sh -c 'tldr tar' > /dev/null

####> Finishing up...
#
# CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_MOUSE_CLICKS IS HALF OF ISSUE #77 AND MUST NOT BE READ ON ITS OWN.
# The other half is `"tui": "fullscreen"` in files/claude-code/managed-settings.json, which is
# what makes the wheel reach Claude Code at all. Turning that on also turns on the fullscreen
# renderer's `copyOnSelect`, which DEFAULTS TO TRUE: a drag then writes the selection out over
# OSC 52 and toasts "copied N chars to tmux buffer - paste with <prefix>p". Measured. This
# configuration sets `prefix None`, so that is instructions to press a key that does not exist,
# about text the student cannot reach -- the same class of failure a9336c0 deleted tmux's own
# copy path over, arriving through the application instead of through tmux.
#
# THE NAME IS A TRAP: it does not disable clicks. It switches Claude Code's tracking from
# `?1000h ?1002h ?1003h ?1006h` to `?1000h ?1006h`, so button press and release are still
# reported -- clicking a link or a button inside the agent still works, which is what
# tmux.conf's MouseDown1Pane binding exists for -- and only MOTION is dropped. Measured both
# ways in the container: mouse_any_flag stays 1 so the wheel guard still forwards, while a drag
# and a double-click each produce zero paste buffers and no toast. SHIFT+drag is untouched,
# because it never reaches the application in the first place.
#
# An internal, undocumented variable, so it can vanish without notice. Nothing can assert that
# Claude Code still honours it -- there is no non-interactive readout of the renderer or of the
# mouse mode (`claude config` is not a subcommand and `claude doctor` reports installation
# health only), and the behavioural check needs a logged-in session. tests/MANUAL.md carries it.
ENV LANG=en_US.UTF-8 \
    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_MOUSE_CLICKS=1 \
    LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 \
    PATH=/home/student/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/sbin:/bin:/sbin \
    EDITOR=nano \
    VISUAL=nano \
    PAGER=less \
    LESS=FRX \
    PIP_BREAK_SYSTEM_PACKAGES=1 \
    BROWSER=/usr/local/bin/open-url
# PIP_BREAK_SYSTEM_PACKAGES=1 is what makes `pip3 install <anything>` work here at all, and it is
# set for the STUDENT rather than for the build. Ubuntu ships PEP 668's
# /usr/lib/python3.14/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED and pip obeys it, so without this variable
# `pip3 install requests` fails with `error: externally-managed-environment` -- and so does
# `pip3 install --user requests`, which is the part nobody expects, since --user writes a
# directory apt never touches. What is left without it is a venv or a flag named
# --break-system-packages, and neither is a good first impression of a language. With it, pip
# says "Defaulting to user installation" and lands in ~/.local, no sudo and no flags. Issue #44.
#
# NOT `rm /usr/lib/python3.14/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED`, which is exactly the same thing to pip and
# does not survive: the marker is a plain file -- not a conffile -- in libpython3.14-stdlib, and
# `apt reinstall libpython3.14-stdlib` puts it straight back. Verified. A Python stdlib security
# update mid-quarter would silently re-break pip, with nothing to point at.
#
# An ENV rather than inline, which is the OPPOSITE of the PIPX_HOME/PIPX_BIN_DIR rule further up
# and for the same underlying reason: those must not reach the student's shell, and this one is
# useless unless it does. 10-static.sh asserts both halves.
#
# `sudo pip3 install` still refuses, and that is the desirable half rather than an oversight:
# sudo's env_reset strips this variable, so root's pip still honours the marker and the
# apt-managed tree stays protected. The student's own ~/.local needs no ceremony; /usr/lib does.
#
# HOST and FLASK_RUN_HOST are deliberately NOT set, and were REMOVED rather than left in
# place. They existed for one reason: podman's forwarder never delivered to the container's
# loopback, so every server had to bind 0.0.0.0, and these nudged the few frameworks that
# read an environment variable into doing it. The launcher now forwards each course port into
# the container's own loopback over ssh, so localhost, 127.0.0.1 and 0.0.0.0 all work and
# there is nothing left to nudge.
#
# Leaving them would have been harmless in the sense that 0.0.0.0 still works. That is
# exactly why they had to go: an environment variable that silently changes what a student's
# server binds to, for a reason that no longer exists, is the kind of thing that survives for
# years and then confuses whoever finds it. They also never worked as advertised -- vite
# reads no HOST variable at all and Next.js reads HOSTNAME, so between them they covered
# little more than react-scripts while vite owned two of the six port ranges.
#
# 50-image.sh asserts their ABSENCE, so re-adding one breaks a test rather than slipping in.
#
# GIT_EDITOR is deliberately NOT set either. With GIT_EDITOR, core.editor, VISUAL and EDITOR all
# unset, `git var GIT_EDITOR` returns `vi` and /usr/bin/vi is vim.tiny — so `git commit`
# with no -m strands a novice in a modal editor with no visible way out. The VS Code
# extension hides this today by exporting GIT_EDITOR=true; we fix it properly instead.
#
# LESS=FRX is git's own default (PAGER_ENV in git's Makefile): F quits if the output fits
# one screen, R passes ANSI colour through, X leaves output on screen instead of wiping
# it. Setting it here extends that to man, journalctl and a student's own `... | less`.
#
# HOST/FLASK_RUN_HOST are a partial measure only. Verified: vite reads NO host env var
# (server.host defaults to 'localhost', settable only by config or --host) and Next.js
# reads HOSTNAME, not HOST. Enforcing the bind-address rule is deliberately deferred;
# see CONTAINER-DESIGN.md § Ports.

USER student
WORKDIR /home/student/projects

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/cs193v-entrypoint"]
